# ── LINE ENDINGS ARE LF EVERYWHERE, INCLUDING ON A WINDOWS CHECKOUT ──────────
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE OF A RED CI RUN, NOT AS HOUSEKEEPING (2026-09-01).
# `node 22 / windows-latest` failed on
# `apps/desktop-ui/src/components/app-shell/frame-palette.test.ts` with
#
#     expected '"use client";\r\n\r\nimport type { Re…'
#       to contain 'SECTION_PANEL_GROUND =\n  "border bor…'
#
# The test is a SOURCE PIN: it `readFileSync`s a real source file and asserts
# the file contains a multi-line literal. On a Windows runner git's default
# `core.autocrlf=true` checks that file out with CRLF, so every `\n` in every
# expected literal misses by one invisible byte. Nothing was wrong with the
# code — the assertion was comparing against a differently-encoded copy of it.
#
# ⚠ IT IS A CLASS, NOT ONE TEST. There are ~27 source-pinning tests in this
# repo (`grep -rl readFileSync` over `src/` and `apps/desktop-ui/src/`), and
# EVERY ONE of them is a latent Windows failure the moment it asserts across a
# line break. Fixing them one `.replace(/\r\n/g, "\n")` at a time is a bug
# waiting on the next author to forget; fixing the CHECKOUT fixes all of them
# and every one written after this.
#
# ⚠ ZERO CHURN, VERIFIED BEFORE ADDING: `git grep -Il $'\r'` matched NOTHING at
# the time this landed, i.e. every text file in the repo is already LF in the
# index. This changes what a Windows working tree gets, never what is stored.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's own binary detection — do not replace it with a blunt
# `text`, which would corrupt images, fonts and the `.icns` app icon.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so no heuristic ever gets a vote on them.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.dmg  binary
*.node binary
